{"chunk_id": "ab4e1fe8529d", "text": "Rama, seated among his sorrowful companions, confesses the anguish troubling his heart: public gossip about Sita\u2019s purity. Although he recounts how Sita proved her innocence after Ravana\u2019s abduction\u2014entering the sacred fire and being vindicated by Agni, the gods, and celestial witnesses\u2014Rama fears the corrosive power of public dishonor. As a king of the Ikshvaku line, he believes a ruler must remain beyond reproach, even at terrible personal cost. Tormented by the criticism of the people, he concludes that reputation and royal duty outweigh his private certainty of Sita\u2019s innocence. The speech builds toward a tragic decision: he will separate from Sita despite loving her and knowing she is pure. The passage reveals the painful tension between dharma as kingly duty and personal loyalty.\nRama, distressed by public criticism of Sita despite her proven innocence, tells Lakshmana he must take her away to a forest hermitage.\nThemes: public honor vs personal love, royal duty, social reputation, sacrifice, Sita's innocence", "vector": [0.012226011604070663, -0.009063063189387321, 0.0009351986227557063, -0.00933340098708868, -0.012874821200966835, 0.005143170244991779, -0.027844758704304695, 0.002047806279733777, -0.020451027899980545, -0.019275059923529625, 0.013935895636677742, 0.007353178691118956, 0.0045923576690256596, -0.0004853402206208557, -0.00925905816257, 0.0028858522418886423, 0.044524580240249634, 0.0017571934731677175, 0.03179168328642845, -0.00907658040523529, 0.002235352760180831, -0.014692841097712517, -0.009373951703310013, 0.009657805785536766, -0.019788701087236404, 0.002012324519455433, 0.028331365436315536, -0.034576158970594406, 0.016774438321590424, -0.02825026400387287, -0.010205239057540894, -0.0217216145247221, -0.009509120136499405, -0.0007206182926893234, -0.04087502136826515, 0.003272772766649723, -0.0036056258250027895, -0.008860310539603233, 0.003254187060520053, -0.0058257719501